**Problem 1 · warm-up**

```sql
CREATE TABLE stock (sku TEXT PRIMARY KEY, qty INTEGER);
INSERT INTO stock VALUES ('pen', 10);
INSERT INTO stock VALUES ('ink', 7) ON CONFLICT(sku) DO UPDATE SET qty = excluded.qty;
INSERT INTO stock VALUES ('pen', 4) ON CONFLICT(sku) DO UPDATE SET qty = excluded.qty;
INSERT INTO stock VALUES ('ink', 3) ON CONFLICT(sku) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO stock VALUES ('pen', 1) ON CONFLICT(sku) DO UPDATE SET qty = qty + 1;
SELECT sku, qty FROM stock ORDER BY sku;
```

Trace the five INSERT statements in order.

<solution>1: pen 10. 2: no row for ink, so a plain insert: ink 7. 3: pen conflicts; excluded is the row that was proposed for insertion, (pen, 4), so qty becomes 4. 4: ink conflicts, DO NOTHING: ink stays 7. 5: pen conflicts, qty = 4 + 1 = 5. Final rows: ink 7, pen 5.</solution>

<ask type="problem">What each INSERT does, and the two rows the final SELECT returns. Work it out before you run the block.</ask>

**Problem 3 · same level**

```sql
PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;
CREATE TABLE authors (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE books (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL,
                    author_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES authors(id));
INSERT INTO authors VALUES (1, 'Achebe'), (2, 'Woolf');
INSERT INTO books VALUES (10, 'Arrow of God', 1), (11, 'Orlando', 2);
DELETE FROM authors WHERE id = 1;
```

<solution>The DELETE is rejected with "FOREIGN KEY constraint failed": a book still references author 1, and a foreign key with no ON DELETE clause takes the default action, NO ACTION, which refuses the delete. Both tables keep 2 rows.</solution>

<ask type="problem">What does the DELETE do, and how many rows do authors and books hold afterwards?</ask>

An upsert is an INSERT that says what to do when the key already exists. With DO NOTHING the new row is dropped:

```sql
CREATE TABLE page_views (page TEXT PRIMARY KEY, n INTEGER);
INSERT INTO page_views VALUES ('home', 1) ON CONFLICT(page) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO page_views VALUES ('home', 1) ON CONFLICT(page) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO page_views VALUES ('home', 1) ON CONFLICT(page) DO NOTHING;
SELECT page, n FROM page_views;
```

The SELECT returns one row: home, 1. The second and third INSERT met the existing key and did nothing.

<solution>Replace DO NOTHING with DO UPDATE SET n = n + 1 in the INSERT statements. The first inserts (home, 1); the second and third meet the conflict and add 1 each, so the SELECT returns home, 3.</solution>

<ask type="problem">Change the INSERT statements so that each one counts a view: after running the block the SELECT should return home, 3. Give your statements and say what each of the three does.</ask>

